#{[17592186495278 "For every unit price in \"dollars per item\", there is also a **reciprocal unit price** in \"items per dollar\".\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells fabric for $\\[math]2$ per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\textrm{dollars per yard} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}  \\,\\textrm{yards per dollar} .\n$$\n\nSo, if we have $\\[/math]1,$ then we can buy $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a yard of fabric." #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495279 "A **unit price** is the price of a single item. To compute a unit price, we divide the total cost of a group of items by the number of items in the group.\n\nFor example, suppose a store sells a pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12.$ \n\nTo find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of shirts:\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3  = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[/math]4$ per shirt." #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 "When we compare prices, it is often helpful to know the *unit price*. Knowing it helps us:\n\n- compare different products,\n\n- determine the total cost for a given amount,\n\n- decide which option is the better deal.\n\nFor example, suppose Lena wants to buy some apples. The price of apples at a nearby market is $\\[math]1.20/\\mathrm{lb}.$ How much would Lena pay for $4$ pounds of apples?\n\nThe unit price is $\\[/math]1.20$ per pound.  \n\nSince Lena is buying $4$ pounds, we multiply the unit price by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.20\\times 4 & =4.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lena would pay $\\[math]4.80$ for $4$ pounds of apples.\n\nWhenever we know the unit price and the quantity, we can find the total cost by multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Unit Price} \\times \\text{Quantity}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 "Unit prices can be used to quantify how good of a deal a particular purchase is.\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells two packs of shirts:\n\n- $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12$\n\n- $5$ shirts for $\\[/math]15$\n\nTo determine which pack is the better deal, we can compare their unit prices.\n\n- For the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- For the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n15 \\div 5 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]3.$\n\nNow we interpret the unit prices in the context of the problem.\n\n- In the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- In the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]3.$\n\nBased on the above, we conclude that the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15$ is the better deal because it has a lower unit price (meaning that each shirt costs less)." #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186497490 "The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. \n\nFor example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/48/48b416b9dffbf6e3e746e3fa8f90ec8043f937fe612e36e1219b5e9e8c368f22.png)\n\n How to find the total length of the border of one slice of pie?\n\nThe border of the slice of pie will consist of two parts:\n\n- the top and the bottom parts, which consist of two radii;\n\n- the right part (circular part), which consists of one-sixth of the circumference.\n\nFirst, let's find the length of the circular part. The formula for the circumference of a circle is ![](images/63/636374af24eb4d5c435de97b8e1ac460148deba4916b0954e3d1d2f7a364f9d7.svg) where ![](images/31/3122e2ea79ab0512edfce5c875a099de1a7067581b658d1b8fc9c105277aed36.svg) is the radius. Therefore, we have \n![](images/15/15bff81cb45d3df680e782e69534d2489388dfbcfd12b3cd579298d03f8bd452.svg)\n\nFinally, the length of the border is\n![](images/15/15037adbdea6b21d54991917b5dad6108c60257fd8f509300642343a101ebeeb.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499634 "Now, let's see how we can find a value given the ratio and another value.\n\nSuppose at a school library, there are some science books and $396$ history books. The ratio of the number of science books to the number of history books is $3: 4.$ By rounding the number of history books to the nearest ten, approximate the number of science books in the library.\n\nFirst, let's approximate the number of history books:\n\n$$\n396 \\longrightarrow 400\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the number of science books to the number of history books is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nHere, \n\n- the numerator represents the number of science books, and\n\n- the denominator represents the number of history books.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $400$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times 100}{4 \\times 100} = \\dfrac{300}{400}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $300$ science books in the library." #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499635 "When we **estimate** a root, we choose one nearby decimal value and check that it is reasonable. Bounds tell us the interval; the estimate tells us which value in that interval is closest.\n\nLet's estimate ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place. First, we find nearby perfect cubes:\n![](images/bc/bca4e36ccfb33070d91ecf1f82eab6ce7d8e06f71d05000633a58ac03ce19107.svg)\nso\n![](images/cd/cdf283b9d6e8644576f4e97abe9203e4115c00fd43314a2323706d1c643e9c0b.svg)\n\n![](images/36/3636292deb3377b1dfe99da068f300f7dba4ce7fa6acd22164150451dc396ee1.png)\n\nBecause ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) is closer to ![](images/c6/c64ba31f35ed2d1f23f3827854effc6461b82c7cfa7f27694bf545acb565c90a.svg) than to ![](images/f9/f9e10c6abbe30486481349d2f58357e01d37bfd670ed37a29313e8556b57fe2c.svg) we expect ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) to be closer to ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) than to ![](images/9f/9fc073d1e6ebde82eb949a875e40be00b9196e9b14473ee13fa47a26a98d8450.svg) Now, we test nearby tenths:\n![](images/47/4703877c1fc434e00d2fc781d77473c9ce8f775257b953203baa76d914c2458b.svg)\n\nSo, ![](images/5d/5dfbe3c8f263be79d4c240a343aeef3ed741dc5d9a72b0b8c21b38cd9548a66c.svg) To decide which tenth is the better estimate, compare distances from ![](images/3b/3b3b7808b8023e6edb41eb64eb15e0198ce6843ff72ec8c44e79786e8932dc84.svg)\n![](images/60/60b754ce1636a8fee75de533c7961f498ac37fd7f6b471971e420985069f3c22.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/bc/bc98a2fdf686f851fd343e6cca8ebf25186c6d4fe401df573939f8e5e1d5d044.svg) is closer to ![](images/92/9252231dcb743cf6f0c6df56fdcc7e3f30a9c4d371ad30332ac7a5f93b38b3e4.svg) the root is closer to ![](images/10/106a07431c2edab74f94a851a8f9d6d4c67fb6e29b7c2090e316e5c2032195e3.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/5d/5d0fdcc02424b0794c647227784e86791079bbdbc184868c2bbbdec3ddcf86cb.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499636 "Some roots are perfect. For example, ![](images/38/389d3c3681c9e50a78b79c73049365330340f56d1dd87adfa83ed9cc3e3eefed.svg) and ![](images/47/4788ae8534df07b8199121e142f69086fd3aec0f87f201fcf4997831c0b60002.svg) Other roots are not whole numbers, but we can still describe where they belong.\n\nAn **approximation** is a nearby value that helps us understand the size of a number. To approximate roots, we compare the number under the radical to nearby perfect powers.\n\nFor a square root, we compare to perfect squares. Since ![](images/96/96d11100098752a9a927d8b25751f689094ffee73faec61b29b79e0afa375a42.svg) we know\n![](images/49/49fbd1142d93edad07ad98c73bc1dafb04d1e8b5a4820274f51d8cee4e3dfbd3.svg)\n\nFor a cube root, we compare to perfect cubes. Since ![](images/2d/2de677effff1f2ce5ed73724bfdd3bead3f0baab3bac33ed272af03361ec3573.svg) we know\n![](images/7e/7ec574473402909905dc4960cca1ab56bb5790c5fcd80f4ad78cd3b9954dc925.svg)\n\nSo, the main idea is to find familiar benchmark roots first. Once we know the root’s whole-number interval, we can order roots, refine to decimal intervals, and choose reasonable estimates." #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499637 "When we order irrational roots, we can often compare their whole-number intervals first. A root between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) is greater than any root between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nSuppose we want to identify the greatest number in this list:\n![](images/3b/3b5235ec98b311f2a082eeb8a35ba2e606f452a5467707a2125ce3f27fd26484.svg)\n\nWe first bracket each root.\n\n- ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/a3/a34f852d765024d8ae773b8debb02adc77fc371c656b049104a9330dc604f5a4.svg)\n\n- ![](images/5a/5aa880157a445c826aa5e51bfbdf4f3c5ab83afdadf97fc6f533aa6103d28640.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/64/6442e0ee53f3ae690132986dfe60822810ae2f586296bccecba0ab6a07c871db.svg)\n\n- ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) lies between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/39/39c9d5c6b02f72e65180a1a782af0404d25977c3627c5778e1bcb676286506c4.svg) since ![](images/5b/5bf4ba43a56be76e42af12ac6d00913b93191cb348019c518a60bb6490d412ac.svg)\n\n- ![](images/55/556af15807fb8546143a7adb4457089c3f367764397c6cf94654f30200177aba.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/65/65aec0b2f64e5e7991650650102102099e40d0fa3f33b9951e7f42d02bbba8d2.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/f4/f4638dd2c251dd734b480e1bcaedeb9e1bee07e2f03ff4c85de10287cae9957b.svg)\n\nThe greatest value must be one of the roots between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) so it is either ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) or ![](images/8b/8bdb2119f2b5ebc9f21dcc182d6b68e8e964156168a62cacb6048ca84b6e4dbf.svg) Since ![](images/eb/eb86e1418a307c988f3b246894cfdf9d0e9c8bbceda1641fb831d30fe9b6fd3b.svg) we know ![](images/45/45ee1b9d94d27812c364bf34c46e918d9c2df4b254adbf86932c2d350cde0822.svg)\n\nTherefore, the greatest number is ![](images/8b/8bdb2119f2b5ebc9f21dcc182d6b68e8e964156168a62cacb6048ca84b6e4dbf.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** We can compare radicands directly only when the roots are the same kind. For example, comparing ![](images/5a/5aa880157a445c826aa5e51bfbdf4f3c5ab83afdadf97fc6f533aa6103d28640.svg) and ![](images/04/04217800f61f8a2b9dfc2b4efb67a08c1d9f5ef7e365a5ddae4d163146bc0158.svg) is safe because both are square roots." #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499638 "**Decimal bounds** are nearby decimal values that trap a root in a smaller interval, such as between two consecutive tenths.\n\nLet's find the two consecutive tenths between which ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) lies. First, we find the whole-number bounds:\n![](images/67/670a358c4cfbfff0330b32c236f83d3cf1b52475f6c624873a54bc335aa11b26.svg)\nso\n![](images/0f/0f2da556fe760c541b67a1f219a28784b92ab589f10c67d8bdefe8d4a27548e3.svg)\n\nNow, we test tenths between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) by squaring them. Since ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) is close to ![](images/3f/3f1007009d0a0d180a52510e7c9ce724113d2deb10051c26452457286845c91c.svg) we can test ![](images/52/521ed5d9c72e7dc13edf7e79df2272294cd869bd5de324f0f7828e2c888c326c.svg) and ![](images/22/227c2d0283023dacad68c1e78194ec0be80b93d52bffea8bd61b5bafc99102df.svg)\n![](images/66/662e926243f31e64daaa74edc74dd4ef9d78e4d24260995fa38db61be6d5cecb.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/e2/e2574f61b59164e433af2aeeb4c0dd160bf84436387873b2af855fa2770b5753.svg) falls between these square values, we have\n![](images/26/26bc3e1ccf6f626db492303aa9d1493c863cf8a10a2e1e46151f1c89ce86c0bd.svg)\n\nTaking square roots gives\n![](images/d1/d1aa32699a90c28680f86d47dca927f92f769ed1e879af7972a3a97030df30db.svg)\n\n![](images/47/4729d848e2e66944d7e2da9f8fc7655a93a59ac5ee20e9454f95b583b39cd8ee.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/75/75690cbc152b11d764581bab44be87acabbbf69ccaca10786de7fd41462db7c1.svg) lies between the consecutive tenths ![](images/52/521ed5d9c72e7dc13edf7e79df2272294cd869bd5de324f0f7828e2c888c326c.svg) and ![](images/22/227c2d0283023dacad68c1e78194ec0be80b93d52bffea8bd61b5bafc99102df.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499639 "We can use what we know about rounding numbers to approximate ratios.\nWe round the quantities to a given degree first, before setting up the ratio.\n\nLook at the following table, which shows the number of girls in a ballet school with blonde or black hair.\n\n| Blonde Hair | Black Hair |\n| --- | --- |\n| $33$ | $58$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, let's determine the approximate ratio of girls with blonde hair to girls with black hair.\n\nFirst, let's approximate the number of girls with blonde or black hair by rounding to the nearest ten.\n\n- Number of girls with blonde hair: $33 \\longrightarrow 30$\n\n- Number of girls with black hair: $58 \\longrightarrow 60$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls with blonde hair to the number of girls with black hair is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{60}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{60 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls with blonde hair to the number of girls with black hair is approximately $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499640 "**Bounds** are values that trap a number between a lower value and an upper value. For roots, **whole-number bounds** are consecutive whole numbers that the root lies between.\n\nTo find whole-number bounds, we compare the number under the radical to nearby perfect squares or perfect cubes.\n\nLet's locate ![](images/db/dbd2aff681e9d97891999f08502f4f6e7f41f487b237d6c90e18afa1f77755ef.svg) The nearby perfect squares are ![](images/24/24341571e162e3b4e2ca76590f0ed2e6d8b180365fba30c3fb9ea6041d38179a.svg) and ![](images/3c/3cba1142c4b937d3598aaaed096f8b95178d8c45baf39fb7f9f02f21a21480df.svg) because\n![](images/09/09aad9428a24389e5a19034319e2737d08cf5167bf424e1b78cf0f0e69e49102.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/11/119ef80e819318a00b93e3c34aaca245545c965604be3489dd3529df1232a453.svg) is between these perfect squares, we have\n![](images/8f/8fbdaf9d9bd26df7fe6d1c1ad4403f07ba47a94f1a7f8b0d4e728b6865a7cb23.svg)\n\nTaking the square root of each part gives\n![](images/c9/c9753a4537cde93f9b0a994a0c50bdc6528dba6926bdd021d0cc2b54732277ba.svg)\n\n![](images/44/448579015986d36689743ffeadb6a8b05c96b917778d63a0de7e4a2dd0487370.png)\n\nSo, ![](images/e8/e8219f12c397796f3e761fb39940934f3f6ff51cb7dd3656018be76bcf7db1ba.svg) lies between the consecutive whole numbers ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) The same idea works for cube roots, except we compare to nearby perfect cubes instead." #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502215 "Sometimes we know the total change in a quantity and want to find how much the quantity changes during each equal time period.\n\nIn these situations, we use division.\n\nFor example, suppose a total of $\\[math]24$ is withdrawn from a bank account over $6$ days in equal amounts.\n\nSince money is withdrawn, the total change in the balance is $-\\[/math]24$.\n\nTo find the change each day, we divide the total change by the number of days:\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6 = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balance changes by $-\\[math]4$ each day." #edb/ref 13194139535388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502216 "Some real-world situations involve a quantity changing by the same amount repeatedly.\n\nWhen the same change occurs repeatedly, we can represent the total change using multiplication.\n\nFor example, suppose a submarine begins at sea level and descends $2\\,\\text{m}$ each minute for $4$ minutes.\n\nSince descending represents a decrease in elevation, the change each minute is $-2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThe total change can be written as repeated subtraction:\n\n![](images/bd/bd6e5218d475c46dd7f0ac578340124954d00fa940c81e32bffa49638b0e79c3.svg)\n\nThis repeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} \\times (-2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ minutes, the submarine's elevation is $-8\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521659 "Now, consider the following diagram.\n\n![](images/02/02d8744bbc39ac9ed10d276071599f9008185023ebdfc3aaf97aebd498d82629.png)\n\nLet's solve for $x$ given the figure above.\n\nFrom the diagram, we have two complementary angles, so\n\n$$\n46^\\circ + (8x + 4^\\circ) = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{46}^{∘}+(8x+{4}^{∘}) & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 8x+{50}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 8x & ={40}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535595 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
